# 11. Why / How / Difference Questions

### ⭐ Why doesn't an index always improve performance?

Because the optimizer compares possible execution strategies.

Suppose:

```text
Table = 1,000,000 rows

Query returns:
800,000 rows
```

Using an index may involve:

```text
Index
 ↓
Many matching entries
 ↓
Many row lookups
 ↓
High I/O
```

A sequential scan may be cheaper.

Therefore:

> **Index exists ≠ Index must be used.**

The optimizer chooses an execution plan based on the available information and estimated cost. ([MySQL Developer Zone][2])

